# Git Commands – One-Page Theory Notes

## 1. What is Git?

Git is a distributed version control system used to track changes in source code, manage project versions, and collaborate with other developers.

Git vs GitHub: Git is a version control tool; GitHub is an online platform for hosting Git repositories.

## 2. Important Git Commands

| Command                                  | Theory / Purpose                                                           |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `git --version`                          | Checks the installed Git version.                                          |
| `git config --global user.name "Name"`   | Sets the global username.                                                  |
| `git config --global user.email "email"` | Sets the global email address.                                             |
| `git init`                               | Initializes a new Git repository.                                          |
| `git clone URL`                          | Copies an existing remote repository to the local system.                  |
| `git status`                             | Shows the current state of files.                                          |
| `git add filename`                       | Adds a specific file to the staging area.                                  |
| `git add .`                              | Stages all changes in the current directory.                               |
| `git commit -m "message"`                | Saves staged changes as a commit.                                          |
| `git log`                                | Displays commit history.                                                   |
| `git diff`                               | Shows changes that have not been staged.                                   |
| `git branch`                             | Lists local branches.                                                      |
| `git branch branch-name`                 | Creates a new branch.                                                      |
| `git switch branch-name`                 | Switches to another branch.                                                |
| `git merge branch-name`                  | Combines changes from another branch into the current branch.              |
| `git remote -v`                          | Displays configured remote repositories.                                   |
| `git push`                               | Uploads local commits to a remote repository.                              |
| `git pull`                               | Fetches and integrates changes from a remote repository.                   |
| `git fetch`                              | Downloads remote updates without integrating them into the current branch. |
| `git rm filename`                        | Removes a file and stages its deletion.                                    |

## 3. Git Workflow

Working Directory

Create or modify files

Staging Area — `git add`

Select changes to save

Local Repository — `git commit`

Record changes locally

Remote Repository — `git push`

Upload commits to GitHub

## 4. Important Concepts

- Repository: A location where project files and their version history are stored.
- Commit: A snapshot of staged changes.
- Staging Area: An intermediate area where changes are selected before committing.
- Branch: An independent line of development.
- Merge Conflict: Occurs when Git cannot automatically reconcile conflicting changes.
- Remote: A version of the repository hosted elsewhere, such as on GitHub.
- HEAD: A reference to the currently checked-out commit.
- `.gitignore`: A file that specifies which untracked files Git should ignore.

## 5. Basic Command Sequence

```
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin <repository-url>
git push -u origin main
```

Remember: `add` stages changes → `commit` saves them locally → `push` uploads them.
